// Domain vocabulary over FORMULAS, not strings — demo 2's whole point.
//
// The WRN case study's `01-onco.esl` declares
//
//     data onco:RequiresActivity : core:string -> core:string -> Prop
//
// and its claims read `RequiresActivity("WRN", "helicase")`. But the parser does not produce
// strings: it produces typed terms over real classes. The proposition for
// «MSI cancer models required the helicase activity of WRN» contains
//
//     ConstRef(umlscui:C0388246)   the WRN gene concept
//     ConstRef(umlscui:C0920283)   the helicase concept
//
// So a string-typed consequent forces the bridge to relate a FORMULA to two STRING LITERALS, and
// nothing in the system relates the literal "helicase" to the class `umlscui:C0920283`. The kernel
// cannot check that step — it is pure assertion, and it discards exactly the typed structure the
// parser was built to produce.
//
// Typing the predicates at `Set` — the sort the parser's own arguments inhabit
// (`ontology:compound_kind : lexicon:Entity -> Set -> Prop`) — makes the bridge relate two formulas
// that SHARE SUBTERMS. `eigenius-reasoning`'s `BridgedClaimGrader` additionally refuses any argument
// class that does not occur in the parsed proposition, so a bridge can narrow what a sentence means
// but cannot introduce a subject the sentence never mentioned.
//
// These live in their own namespace rather than re-typing `onco:`: the WRN chain's committed
// conclusions use the string form, and migrating them is separate work.

// ── WHERE THESE PREDICATE NAMES COME FROM ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// All three are declared here, in a demo namespace, and are drawn from no published ontology.
// That is a carry-over. `RequiresActivity` and `DispensableActivity` were invented for the WRN
// reproduction experiment (experiments/publications/wrn-helicase/chain/01-onco.esl, 2026-06-18),
// when the chain had no grounded vocabulary to draw on. This file re-typed them at `Set` for
// demo 2 and did not revisit where the NAMES come from: the migration grounded the ARGUMENTS
// (`"WRN"` → `umlscui:C0388246`) and left the PREDICATES where they were.
//
// ESL has no slot for this on a `data` declaration — the body accepts constructors only — so
// unlike `literature-rules.esl`, whose rationale is committed and visible to a reader of the
// GRAPH, what follows lives only in this source file. Closing that is a language change, not
// something to work around with an ad-hoc annotation resource here.
//
// The ARGUMENTS carry the same debt with a structural excuse. `exonuclease` is `wn:n14606137`, a
// WordNet synset — a general-English dictionary sense — where GO:0004527 "exonuclease activity"
// is the canonical term; `helicase` is `umlscui:C0920283` where GO:0004386 exists. But a bridge
// may only name classes the PARSE produced (`BridgedClaimGrader` refuses any other), and the
// parser's lexicon is WordNet + UMLS. So the vocabulary a lift can reach is capped by the
// vocabulary the parser can see: grounding in GO is a LEXICON change, not a bridge change. That
// cap is the price of the guarantee that a lift cannot introduce a subject the sentence never
// mentioned.

namespace core = "urn:eigenius:core";
namespace onco2 = "urn:eigenius:demo:onco-typed";

// INVENTED, and legitimately so. "This model requires this gene's activity for viability" is a
// research-level dependency claim. RO has no relation for it; the nearest, RO:0002502 `depends
// on`, is generic. Declaring new vocabulary is what a chain is for — the debt is only that
// nothing here said it was new.
data onco2:RequiresActivity : Set -> Set -> Prop {
}

// INVENTED (the complement of RequiresActivity), and UNUSED by this demo — only
// demo/prose-to-chain references it. Carried over with the other two.
data onco2:DispensableActivity : Set -> Set -> Prop {
}

// The measured property the literature rule's antecedent speaks of.
//
// DUPLICATES A STANDARD RELATION, and should cite it once a relation ontology is on the chain.
// RO:0002215 `capable of` is "a relation between a material entity (such as a cell) and a
// process, in which the material entity has the ability to carry out the process" — which is what
// this predicate means, and it is the relation GO itself uses to link gene products to molecular
// functions. Unlike the two above, this one was introduced fresh for demo 2 (it appears nowhere
// in the WRN experiment), so it duplicated an existing term rather than inheriting the habit.
data onco2:HasActivity : Set -> Set -> Prop {
}
